 Taliban leadership and therefore  the question how important is restoring  women's rights in Afghanistan for  Pakistan I mean it's important but I  don't think that it's a priority right  now for Pakistan uh Pakistan right now  is under threat from uh terrorism  activities of Pakistani Taliban it's a  group that has for years attacked  Pakistani State its people children and  since Afghan Taliban took over in  Afghanistan these attacks have increased  and Pakistan believes that Pakistani  Taliban and these militants are based in  Afghanistan and they are planning these  attacks from the Afghan territory and I  remember in August 2021 I was an  Islamabad  took over  um some military officials were very  jubilant and happy that now Pakistan  will have a friendly neighbor in its  western border and of course Pakistan  already has a hostile neighbor on its  eastern border but the reality has been  that western border has been uh has seen  a lot of problems and there have been  attacks on Pakistan territory I I think  number one message that henarabanikar  gave to Afghan Taliban was that they  want help of Afghan Taliban encountering  this threat of terrorism that Pakistan  believes is coming from the afghan's  wife aside from the foreign policy  deliberations I understand that you  yourself were in Kabul at the Ministry  of Foreign Affairs in 2012 what was it  like then yes so I was about part of an  journalism fellowship program and I  stayed in Kabul for 10 days and I  remember that at the Ministry of Foreign  Affairs we were welcomed by these young  women foreign Ministry officers and they  told us about what kind of Afghanistan  they want what kind of foreign policy  they were pursuing and what kind of  relations they want with Afghanistan I  also went to different other government  Ministries and to also to a news channel  told the news and there were women  participants there so women were very  active part of the society and that is  completely missing right now when I see  visuals of in arabanika meeting with  Afghan Taliban leadership and one thing  I'll quickly say I remember the story  um Afghan and Afghan journalists told me  that he his sister who's actually six  years older than him than him both of  them graduated at the same time because  from 1996 to 2001 when Afghanistan was  under Taliban control his sister  couldn't go to school and unfortunately  that is happening again men after so  many years with so many Afghan women  which is just disappointing and  extremely heartbreak indeed  thank you  and as binis just said the situation is  very different now women human rights  activists for instance have been  arrested and tortured activists such as  who spent 24 days in jail after being  arrested for participating in a women's  rights protest in Kabul in mid-January  this year she's now in Germany as a  refugee and spoke to DW news about her  experience in prison  do you know what punishment we have  chosen for you a Taliban member asked me  one day I said I didn't he said they  would Stone me tell the other girl to  pray we are going to Stone you he said I  started crying and asked him what my  crime was that was the worst time my  biggest wish was to get a helmet so that  I would not get hurt if they stoned me I  checked my clothes to see if there was  something that could protect me from  stoning and I wondered wood stoning hurt  a malformation  activist parwana nishrabi spent 24 days  in a Taliban prison in Afghanistan  her work fighting for women's rights put  her in the Hardline group's Firing Line  since seizing power in August 2021 the  Taliban have severely restricted these  rights  girls have been bought from school and  women face increasingly harsh social  restrictions  after protesting for women's rights in  the Afghan Capital Kabul nishrabi was  arrested  I don't know which Taliban member  slapped my face first  I felt my eardrums were damaged  and he slapped me again and my mouth  started bleeding  